            <ArticleTitle>Pain assessment in nonverbal older adults with advanced dementia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>99-113</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="TOPIC" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Pain assessment is a particular challenge among individuals with advanced dementia who lack the ability to formulate and express their experience of discomfort.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Awareness of pain scales and methods specifically designed for use with nonverbal individuals with dementia is critical to expanded use and testing in clinical settings.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Computerized literature searches using four databases revealed the five observational scales and two caregiver reports methods reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A small number of valid, reliable, and sensitive scales are available for use by nurses and allied health personnel. Each has strengths and limitations and all would benefit from additional testing.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
